  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nIt's a rainy start to the weekend as a cold front sweeps across\nthe Upper Midwest today. We can see winds have already shifted\nout of the northwest for much of Minnesota already, and a \nportion of northwest Wisconsin. This is a good delineation of \nwhere the front currently sits, especially when looking at the \ntemperature difference ahead and behind of the front. Places \nnear Alexandria and Willmar are in the low 40s, while Menomonie \nand Eau Claire are in the upper 50s. The front will serve as the\nmain axis for our second round of rain later today, with the \nfootprint of QPF shifting slightly northwest since the forecast \nthis morning. The greatest amounts still favor southeast \nMinnesota and western Wisconsin, with the mean REFs producing \nbetween 0.5-0.75 inches there. The stretch of counties from\nFreeborn up through Eau Claire even have a chance to get up to \nan inch as well if rain rates are heavy enough. The edge of the \nmeasurable rain generally runs from Fairmont through Mankato, \nthe Twin Cities metro, and up toward St Croix Falls. The \nheaviest rainfall lasts from this afternoon into the evening, \nbut the rain will not fully move east of Eau Claire until around\n6-7AM.\n\nHigh pressure will then move in from Canada starting Sunday,  \nsettling overhead by Monday night. This will bring our coldest \nairmass of the season so far, combined with mostly clear skies \nand generally lighter winds. The latest NBM forecast on Monday \nhas widespread highs at or below freezing. Brr! Sunshine should \nhelp it feel not as chilly during the day, though this will \nlikely be a bit of a slap in the face for many folks who have \nbeen enjoying the warm fall so far.\n\nA couple of weak waves and fronts throughout the week will be\nthe main focus as we warm back up, but are still cold enough \nfor snow to be on the table should we get any precipitation with\neither wave. The main timeframe would be on Wednesday and \nThanksgiving. With Wednesday, the EPS is slightly more \naggressive than the GEFS for snow potential, favoring western \nMN. Forecast soundings do show a shallow layer of saturation in \nthe DGZ, however, the main limiting factor will be the 8-10k \nfeet of dry air below it. The better thermo profile sets up on \nThanksgiving, though the wave is already shifted east and more \ncentered over Wisconsin by this time. The 06z run of the Euro \nAIFs shows an aggressive jump up in snow potential, and bears \nattention to watch if that trend sticks around or if it was just\none run. Model consensus is lacking the further we get into the\nend of the week, but the main takeaways are the mild daytime \ntemps and below freezing lows with continued low chances for \nprecipitation.",
